1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
STEPHANIE: Hello, Trevor.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,510
TREVOR: Hello, Stephanie. You said you wanted to talk about the course I'm taking on literature for children.

3
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:14,420
STEPHANIE: That's right. I'm thinking of doing it next year

4
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:17,550
but I'd like to find out more about it first.

5
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:21,526
TREVOR: OK, well, as you probably know, it's a one-year course.

6
00:00:21,526 --> 00:00:26,225
It's divided into six modules, and you have to take all of them.

7
00:00:26,225 --> 00:00:29,340
One of the most interesting ones, for me, at least

8
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:32,370
was about the purpose of children's literature.

9
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:39,250
STEPHANIE: You mean, whether it should just entertain children or should be educational as well.

10
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:42,726
TREVOR: Right, and whether the teaching should be factual -

11
00:00:42,726 --> 00:00:46,975
giving them information about the world - or ethical, teaching them values.

12
00:00:46,975 --> 00:00:52,383
What's fascinating is that the writer isn't necessarily conscious of the message they're conveying.

13
00:00:52,383 --> 00:00:55,473
For instance, a story might show a child

14
00:00:55,473 --> 00:01:02,426
who has a problem as a result of not doing what an adult has told them to do,

15
00:01:02,426 --> 00:01:05,130
implying that children should always obey adults.

16
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:08,000
STEPHANIE: I see what you mean.

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,657
TREVOR: That module made me realise how important stories are -

18
00:01:11,657 --> 00:01:16,045
they can have a significant effect on children as they grow up.

19
00:01:16,045 --> 00:01:21,896
Actually, it inspired me to have a go at it myself, just for my own interest.

20
00:01:21,896 --> 00:01:25,480
I know I can't compete with the really popular stories

21
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:27,381
like the Harry Potter books -

22
00:01:27,381 --> 00:01:32,500
they're very good, and even young kids like my seven-year-old niece love reading them.

23
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:38,380
STEPHANIE: Mm. I'm very interested in illustrations in stories

24
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:40,500
Is that covered in the course?

25
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:43,780
TREVOR: Yes, there's a module on pictures

26
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:47,400
and how they're sometimes central to the story.

27
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:52,536
STEPHANIE: That's good. I remember some frightening ones I saw as a child

28
00:01:52,536 --> 00:01:57,277
and I can still see them vividly in my mind, years later!

29
00:01:57,277 --> 00:02:02,808
Pictures can be so powerful, just as powerful as words. I've always enjoyed drawing,

30
00:02:02,808 --> 00:02:08,300
so that's the field I want to go into when I finish the course

31
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:11,500
I bet that module will be really helpful.

32
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:13,375
TREVOR: I'm sure it will.

33
00:02:13,375 --> 00:02:16,040
We also studied comics in that module

34
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:20,125
but I'm not convinced of their value, not compared with books.

35
00:02:20,125 --> 00:02:24,860
One of the great things about words is that you use your imagination

36
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:28,000
but with a comic you don't have to.

37
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:34,421
STEPHANIE: But children are so used to visual input - on TV, video games, and so on.

38
00:02:34,421 --> 00:02:39,637
There are plenty of kids who wouldn't even try to read a book,

39
00:02:39,637 --> 00:02:43,650
so I think comics can serve a really useful purpose.

40
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:49,190
TREVOR: You mean, it's better to read a comic than not to read at all?

41
00:02:49,190 --> 00:02:54,730
Yes, I suppose you're right. I just think it's sad when children don't read books.

42
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:59,260
STEPHANIE: What about books for girls and books for boys

43
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:01,680
Does the course go into that?

44
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:04,291
TREVOR: Yes, there's a module on it.

45
00:03:04,291 --> 00:03:07,649
For years, lots of stories, in English, at least,

46
00:03:07,649 --> 00:03:11,580
assumed that boys went out and did adventurous things

47
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:14,364
and girls stayed at home and played with dolls.

48
00:03:14,364 --> 00:03:19,960
I was amazed how many books were targeted at just one sex or the other.

49
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:24,810
Of course this reflects society as it is when the books are written.

50
00:03:24,810 --> 00:03:30,500
STEPHANIE: That's true. So it sounds as though you think it's a good course.

51
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:32,440
TREVOR: Definitely.

52
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:35,620
TREVOR: Have you been reading lots of children's stories

53
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:38,170
to help you decide whether to take the course?

54
00:03:38,170 --> 00:03:43,570
STEPHANIE: Yeah. I've gone as far back as the late seventeenth century,

55
00:03:43,570 --> 00:03:47,170
though I know there were earlier children's stories.

56
00:03:47,170 --> 00:03:50,800
TREVOR: So does that mean you've read Perrault' s fairy tales

57
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:53,300
Cinderella, The Sleeping Beauty, and so on.

58
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:56,860
STEPHANIE: Yes. They must be important

59
00:03:56,860 --> 00:04:00,346
because no stories of that type had been written before,

60
00:04:00,346 --> 00:04:04,750
these were the first. Then there's The Swiss Family Robinson.

61
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:07,000
TREVOR: I haven't read that.

62
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,160
STEPHANIE: The English name makes it sound as

63
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:12,952
though Robinson is the family's surname

64
00:04:12,952 --> 00:04:17,920
but a more accurate translation would be the Swiss Robinsons

65
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:19,753
because it's about a Swiss family

66
00:04:19,753 --> 00:04:25,280
who are shipwrecked, like Robinson Crusoe in the novel of a century earlier.

67
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:28,050
TREVOR: Well I never knew that!

68
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:32,500
STEPHANIE: Have you read Hoffmann's The Nutcracker and the Mouse King?

69
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:37,000
TREVOR: Wasn't that the basis for Tchaikovsky's ballet The Nutcracker?

70
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,000
STEPHANIE: That's right. It has some quite bizarre elements.

71
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,947
TREVOR: I hope you've read Oscar Wilde's The Happy Prince.

72
00:04:46,947 --> 00:04:50,500
It's probably my favourite children's story of all time.

73
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:55,380
STEPHANIE: Mine too! And it's so surprising

74
00:04:55,380 --> 00:04:57,422
because Wilde is best known for his plays,

75
00:04:57,422 --> 00:05:03,883
and most of them are very witty, but The Happy Prince is really moving.

76
00:05:03,883 --> 00:05:08,320
I struggled with Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings

77
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:12,190
three long books, and I gave up after one.

78
00:05:12,190 --> 00:05:15,000
TREVOR: It's extremely popular, though.

79
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,000
STEPHANIE: Yeah, but whereas something like The Happy Prince just carried me along with it.

80
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
The Lord of the Rings took more effort than I was prepared to give it.

81
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,330
TREVOR: I didn't find that - I love it.

82
00:05:28,330 --> 00:05:31,820
STEPHANIE: Another one I've read is War Horse.

83
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:36,040
TREVOR: Oh yes. It's about the First World War, isn't it

84
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:39,320
Hardly what you'd expect for a children's story.

85
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:45,410
STEPHANIE: Exactly, but it's been very successful. Have you read any...
